# ---- Guide ----
helpStepsTitle = How to tell whether the cable is at fault
helpStep1 = 1. Connect the device with the cable and see on Connection whether the speed is capped.
helpStep2 = 2. For USB storage, measure the real throughput on Transfer speed.
helpStep3 = 3. Leave Stability running and flex the cable to see whether it drops.
helpStep4 = 4. For charging, check the wattage on Charging.
helpStep5 = 5. Keep the same device and port, swap in a different cable, and repeat from step 1. That tells you whether the cable or the device is the problem.
helpChargeTitle = When nothing appears at all
helpChargeBody = If the device never shows up on Connection but still charges, the cable has no data wires — it is a charge-only cable. Bundled and cheap cables are often like this. A cable with broken data wires behaves the same way.
helpNamesTitle = USB naming
helpNamesNote = The same speed carries three different names. The connection check shows the current name, the former name and the marketing name together.
colTotal = Total speed
colCurrentName = Current name
colLegacy = Former name
colMarketing = Marketing name
helpNoLegacy = None (new in USB 3.2)
helpLegacyGen1 = USB 3.0 / USB 3.1 Gen 1
helpLegacyGen2 = USB 3.1 Gen 2
helpLimitTitle = What can be identified
helpLimit1 = The per-lane speed — 5Gbps or 10Gbps, that is Gen 1 or Gen 2 — is read in Gbps straight from the device BOS descriptor, so it is identified rather than guessed.
helpLimit2 = USB 3.0 and USB 3.1 Gen 1 are the same 5Gbps specification under two names. Since there is nothing to tell apart, no tool can distinguish them.
helpLimit3 = Windows reports both 10Gbps (Gen 2x1) and 20Gbps (Gen 2x2) as SuperSpeedPlus. When a device declares that it needs two lanes, that is shown, but confirm with the measured value on Transfer speed. Above roughly 1,000 MB/s the link is running at 20Gbps.
helpUnknownTitle = What this app cannot tell you
helpUnknownBody = Software only sees the result of communication with a device attached. Conductor resistance, the contents of the E-Marker chip and shielding quality cannot be measured. To test a cable on its own, you need a dedicated cable tester.
helpColorTitle = Verdict colours
helpColorGood = Green — working as the specification says.
helpColorCaution = Amber — slower than it should be, or a strained setup.
helpColorFault = Red — consider replacing the cable.
helpLangTitle = Adding a language
helpLangBody = Create a lang folder next to the executable, copy one of the language files into it and translate the text. The language appears without rebuilding. The file name is the language code (for example fr.lang).

