# ---- Verdicts ----
vFailedEnum = The device is not being recognised. Suspect a broken or badly seated cable, or a fault in the device. Try another cable and see whether the symptom follows.
vGeneralFail = A connection error occurred. Check the cable and connectors for dirt or deformation.
vOvercurrent = Overcurrent detected. There may be a short inside the cable or the device. Disconnect it now.
vNotEnoughPower = Not enough power. Thin power wires, an over-long cable, or a bus-powered hub are the usual causes.
vNotEnoughBandwidth = Not enough bandwidth. Reduce the number of devices on the same hub.
vNested = Hubs are chained too deeply. Move the device closer to a port on the PC itself.
vLegacyHub = Connected through an older hub. Plugging directly into the PC may fix it.
vEnumerating = Enumeration is still in progress. If it stays this way, suspect a poor cable contact.
vPortUsb2 = The port itself is USB 2.0. Change the port rather than the cable.
vGen2AtGen1 = The device supports 10Gbps (Gen 2) but is linked at 5Gbps (Gen 1). Either the cable is not rated for Gen 2, or the port only goes up to Gen 1. If another cable changes nothing, assume the port is Gen 1.
vUsb3AtUsb2 = A USB 3.x device is running at USB 2.0 speed. Most likely the cable has no USB 3.x wires, or those wires are broken.
vUsb3AtUsb2C = Even between Type-C ends, charge-only and USB 2.0-class cables are common.
vUsb3AtUsb2A = Swap in a USB 3.x cable (the type with a blue connector) and check again.
vTwoLane = The device expects two lanes (Gen 2x2, 20Gbps) but is not linked at that speed. A Type-C cable rated for 20Gbps is required.
vGood10 = Linked at 10Gbps (Gen 2) or above. The cable's high-speed wires are working correctly.
vGood5 = Linked at 5Gbps (Gen 1), which is this device's maximum.
vGoodNormal = Linked at the speed this device is rated for.

